## 1. The problem

At the end of Unit I you had this:

```jsx
<ProfileCard />
<ProfileCard />
```

Both cards show the same text, because the name is hard-coded inside the component file.

**A component you cannot change is not reusable.** You would need forty separate files to show forty students. That defeats the whole point of components.

---

## 2. Props are just function parameters

```js
// an ordinary function
function greet(name) {
  return "Hi " + name;
}
greet("Riya");
```

```jsx
// a component
function Card(props) {
  return <h2>{props.name}</h2>;
}
<Card name="Riya" />
```

**Same idea.** You already know how to pass values into a function. Props are that, written as attributes on a tag.

---

## 3. Send it in, then use it

```jsx
// 1 · sending — App.jsx
<Card name="Riya" />
<Card name="Aarav" />
<Card name="Meera" />
```

```jsx
// 2 · receiving — Card.jsx
function Card(props) {
  return <h2>{props.name}</h2>;
}
```

`props` is **one object** holding everything you sent. Send `name="Riya"` and React hands your function `{ name: "Riya" }`. Read it with `props.name`.

Three cards, three different names, **one component file**.

---

## 4. Destructure the props

```jsx
// works, but repetitive
function Card(props) {
  return <h2>{props.name}</h2>;
}

// ✓ what everyone writes
function Card({ name }) {
  return <h2>{name}</h2>;
}
```

This is the same destructuring you learned in Unit I, applied to the parameter. With several props it saves a lot of typing:

```jsx
function Card({ name, branch, cgpa }) { ... }
```

Use this form from now on — every React codebase does.

---

## 5. Only text uses quotes

```jsx
<Card
  name="Riya"              // text — quotes
  age={20}                 // number — braces
  isTopper={true}          // boolean — braces
  marks={[90, 85, 78]}     // array — braces
/>
```

⚠ **Common bug:** `age="20"` sends the **text** `"20"`, not the number `20`. Then `{age + 5}` gives you `"205"` instead of `25`, because JavaScript joins two strings instead of adding two numbers.

**Rule:** if it is not plain text, wrap it in braces.

---

## 6. Props are read-only

```jsx
// ✗ never do this
function Card({ name }) {
  name = "Changed";
  return <h2>{name}</h2>;
}

// ✓ only read them
function Card({ name }) {
  return <h2>{name}</h2>;
}
```

**Data flows one way — parent to child.** A component never changes the props it was given. If a value needs to change, the parent must change it.

This is the same principle as immutability from Unit I. What handles changing values is **state**, which comes in Unit III.

---

## 7. `children` — whatever you put inside the tag

```jsx
// using it
<Box>
  <h2>Notice</h2>
  <p>Exam on Friday</p>
</Box>
```

```jsx
// Box.jsx
function Box({ children }) {
  return (
    <div className="box">
      {children}
    </div>
  );
}
```

`children` is a prop React fills in automatically with whatever sits between the opening and closing tags.

**Use it for wrappers** — cards, panels, modals. Anything that puts a frame around content it does not know in advance.

---

## 8. Default values

```jsx
function Card({ name, branch = "CSE" }) {
  return <h2>{name} — {branch}</h2>;
}
```

| Usage | Result |
|---|---|
| `<Card name="Riya" branch="ECE" />` | shows ECE |
| `<Card name="Riya" />` | falls back to CSE |

Same default-parameter syntax you already use in ordinary functions.

---

## 9. Type this in

```jsx
// src/StudentCard.jsx
function StudentCard({ name, cgpa }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>CGPA: {cgpa}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default StudentCard;
```

```jsx
// src/App.jsx
import StudentCard from './StudentCard.jsx';

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <StudentCard name="Riya" cgpa={8.7} />
      <StudentCard name="Aarav" cgpa={9.1} />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Now add a third card **without touching `StudentCard.jsx`**. That is reusability.

